        "---\n",
        "title: \"Recurrent Neural Networks\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Dynamical systems, sequence likelihood, and backpropagation through time\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "A bag-of-words classifier treats a sentence as an unordered collection of tokens.  A recurrent neural network treats a sentence as an ordered sequence.  At each time step, the model reads one token, updates an internal state, and uses that state to make predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, an RNN is a parameterized discrete-time dynamical system driven by a sequence of input vectors.  This viewpoint makes the model easier to understand: hidden states are states of a dynamical system, recurrent weights control memory and stability, and backpropagation through time is differentiation through repeated function composition.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. explain why order-sensitive language tasks require sequence models rather than pure bag-of-words models;\n",
        "2. write a vanilla RNN as a nonlinear recurrence;\n",
        "3. interpret hidden states as summaries of past tokens;\n",
        "4. formulate RNNs for sequence classification and language modeling;\n",
        "5. derive the negative log-likelihood for an RNN language model;\n",
        "6. explain backpropagation through time using products of Jacobian matrices;\n",
        "7. connect vanishing and exploding gradients to spectral radius and nonlinear saturation;\n",
        "8. implement a small RNN forward pass and training loop in Python;\n",
        "9. use an interactive visualization to study recurrent memory and stability.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.1 Why recurrence?\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the two short sentences:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{not good}\n",
        "\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n",
        "\\text{good not}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A bag-of-words representation records the same two tokens in both cases.  Therefore a bag-of-words model cannot distinguish them.  More generally, count vectors ignore the order of tokens, so they cannot directly represent syntax, negation scope, local dependencies, or temporal structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $V$ be a vocabulary.  A sentence of length $T$ is an ordered sequence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x=(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_T) \\in V^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A sequence model should process the tokens in order.  Instead of mapping the full sequence immediately to one fixed vector, it constructs a sequence of hidden states\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_1,h_2,\\ldots,h_T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $h_t$ is intended to summarize the prefix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(x_1,\\ldots,x_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The key modeling idea is recursive summarization:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{new summary}\n",
        "=\\text{update}(\\text{old summary},\\text{new token}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is exactly the mathematical structure of a discrete-time dynamical system with external input.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.2 The vanilla RNN as a nonlinear recurrence\n",
        "\n",
        "Let each token $x_t \\in V$ be mapped to an embedding vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e_t = E_{x_t} \\in \\mathbb R^m,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $E\\in \\mathbb R^{d\\times m}$ is an embedding matrix and $d=|V|$.\n",
        "\n",
        "A vanilla RNN defines hidden states $h_t\\in \\mathbb R^r$ by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t = \\phi(W_h h_{t-1}+W_x e_t+b_h),\n",
        "\\qquad t=1,\\ldots,T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $h_0\\in \\mathbb R^r$ is an initial state, often the zero vector;\n",
        "- $W_h\\in \\mathbb R^{r\\times r}$ is the recurrent weight matrix;\n",
        "- $W_x\\in \\mathbb R^{r\\times m}$ maps token embeddings into hidden space;\n",
        "- $b_h\\in \\mathbb R^r$ is a bias vector;\n",
        "- $\\phi$ is a coordinatewise nonlinearity such as $\\tanh$ or ReLU.\n",
        "\n",
        "A common choice is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi(u)=\\tanh(u),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "applied coordinatewise.  Then every coordinate of $h_t$ lies between $-1$ and $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Dimension check.**  If $h_{t-1}\\in \\mathbb R^r$, $e_t\\in \\mathbb R^m$, $W_h\\in \\mathbb R^{r\\times r}$, and $W_x\\in \\mathbb R^{r\\times m}$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_hh_{t-1}+W_xe_t+b_h\\in \\mathbb R^r.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore $h_t\\in \\mathbb R^r$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "The same update rule is applied at every time step.  This **parameter sharing across time** is essential.  It allows the model to process sequences of different lengths and keeps the number of parameters independent of $T$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.3 The state-space viewpoint\n",
        "\n",
        "The RNN recurrence can be written as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t = F_\\theta(h_{t-1},e_t),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_\\theta(h,e)=\\phi(W_hh+W_xe+b_h).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a controlled dynamical system.  The input sequence $e_1,e_2,\\ldots,e_T$ drives the state trajectory\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_0 \\to h_1 \\to h_2 \\to \\cdots \\to h_T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For language, the hidden state has an information-processing interpretation:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t \\approx \\text{a vector summary of the prefix } (x_1,\\ldots,x_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The hidden state is not guaranteed to contain all information about the prefix.  It is a compressed representation of a possibly long discrete sequence.  The quality of this compression depends on the hidden dimension $r$, the parameters, the nonlinearity, the training data, and the task.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**RNN as learned sufficient statistic.**  In ideal probabilistic modeling, one might want a statistic $S_t=S(x_1,\\ldots,x_t)$ such that the future depends on the past only through $S_t$.  An RNN attempts to learn a finite-dimensional vector $h_t$ that plays this role for a prediction task.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.4 Output maps: many-to-one and many-to-many models\n",
        "\n",
        "The hidden states can be used in different ways.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Sequence classification\n",
        "\n",
        "For sentiment classification, topic classification, or spam detection, the input is a sequence and the output is one label.  A common model uses only the final hidden state:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z = W_o h_T+b_o,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p(y\\mid x)=\\operatorname{softmax}(z).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is called a **many-to-one** model because many input tokens produce one output label.\n",
        "\n",
        "The loss for one training example $(x,y)$ is cross-entropy:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\theta;x,y)\n",
        "= -\\log \\widehat p(y\\mid x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### Sequence labeling\n",
        "\n",
        "For part-of-speech tagging or named-entity recognition, the model predicts a label at each time step:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_t = W_o h_t+b_o,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p(y_t\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_t)=\\operatorname{softmax}(z_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a **many-to-many** model.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Language modeling\n",
        "\n",
        "For next-token prediction, the model uses the prefix hidden state to predict the next token:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p(x_{t+1}=j\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_t)\n",
        "= \\operatorname{softmax}(W_o h_t+b_o)_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The RNN language model estimates the conditional distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X_{t+1}\\mid X_1,\\ldots,X_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the recurrent analogue of the $n$-gram models from Chapter 5.  An $n$-gram model keeps a finite symbolic context.  An RNN keeps a finite-dimensional learned vector context.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.5 RNN language-model likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "Let a training sequence be\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x=(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_T).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A language model assigns probability using the chain rule:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_\\theta(x_1,\\ldots,x_T)\n",
        "=\\prod_{t=1}^T P_\\theta(x_t\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In an RNN language model, the conditional probability is computed from the previous hidden state:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_t=\\operatorname{softmax}(W_oh_{t-1}+b_o),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_\\theta(x_t=j\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_{t-1})=(p_t)_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The hidden state is then updated using the observed token $x_t$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t=\\phi(W_hh_{t-1}+W_xE_{x_t}+b_h).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The negative log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\theta;x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\sum_{t=1}^T \\log (p_t)_{x_t}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The average token-level cross-entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{CE}(\\theta;x)\n",
        "=\\frac{1}{T}L(\\theta;x),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the perplexity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL}(\\theta;x)\n",
        "=\\exp(\\operatorname{CE}(\\theta;x)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus training an RNN language model is maximum likelihood estimation, or equivalently empirical risk minimization with cross-entropy loss.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.6 Unrolling the recurrence\n",
        "\n",
        "For a fixed sequence length $T$, the recurrent computation can be unrolled into a deep feedforward network with shared weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_1=F_\\theta(h_0,e_1),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_2=F_\\theta(h_1,e_2)=F_\\theta(F_\\theta(h_0,e_1),e_2),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and so on.  Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_T=F_\\theta(F_\\theta(\\cdots F_\\theta(h_0,e_1)\\cdots,e_{T-1}),e_T).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The same parameters occur at every layer of this unrolled network.  This is why training RNNs is similar to training deep networks, but with additional weight sharing.\n",
        "\n",
        "For sequence classification, the loss may depend only on $h_T$.  For language modeling, the loss depends on many hidden states:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L=\\sum_{t=1}^T \\ell_t(h_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In both cases, gradients must flow backward through the entire chain of recurrent updates.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.7 Backpropagation through time\n",
        "\n",
        "Backpropagation through time, or **BPTT**, is ordinary backpropagation applied to the unrolled recurrent computation graph.\n",
        "\n",
        "To see the main issue, consider how the final state depends on an earlier state.  By the chain rule,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial h_T}{\\partial h_t}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial h_T}{\\partial h_{T-1}}\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial h_{T-1}}{\\partial h_{T-2}}\n",
        "\\cdots\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial h_{t+1}}{\\partial h_t}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For the vanilla RNN,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_s=\\phi(a_s),\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        " a_s=W_hh_{s-1}+W_xe_s+b_h.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The one-step Jacobian is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "J_s\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial h_s}{\\partial h_{s-1}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "D_s W_h,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_s=\\operatorname{diag}(\\phi'(a_s)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial h_T}{\\partial h_t}\n",
        "=\n",
        "J_TJ_{T-1}\\cdots J_{t+1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This product of many matrices is the central mathematical source of vanishing and exploding gradients.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Gradient stability.**  If the norms of the Jacobians are typically less than $1$, products shrink rapidly.  If they are typically greater than $1$, products may grow rapidly.  Long-range learning in vanilla RNNs is difficult because gradients involve long products of Jacobian matrices.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.8 Vanishing and exploding gradients\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose, as a simplified model, that the nonlinear derivative is approximately constant and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "J_s \\approx J.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "J_TJ_{T-1}\\cdots J_{t+1}\\approx J^{T-t}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $J$ has eigenvalues with magnitudes smaller than $1$, then $J^k$ tends to shrink directions as $k$ increases.  If $J$ has eigenvalues with magnitudes larger than $1$, then $J^k$ may grow rapidly in some directions.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a linear recurrence\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t=W_hh_{t-1},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the state is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_T=W_h^T h_0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $W_h$ is diagonalizable as $W_h=Q\\Lambda Q^{-1}$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_h^T=Q\\Lambda^TQ^{-1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The powers of the eigenvalues determine the long-run behavior.  This connects RNN stability to familiar ideas from linear algebra and dynamical systems.\n",
        "\n",
        "For $\tanh$ RNNs, saturation introduces another source of gradient shrinkage.  Since\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{d}{du}\\tanh(u)=1-\\tanh^2(u),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "large values of $|u|$ produce derivatives close to zero.  Even if $W_h$ is not small, saturated nonlinearities can make $D_s$ small.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.9 Truncated BPTT and gradient clipping\n",
        "\n",
        "For long sequences, full BPTT can be expensive and unstable.  Two practical techniques are common.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Truncated backpropagation through time\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead of backpropagating through the entire history, one backpropagates only through a window of length $K$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial h_t}{\\partial h_{t-K}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This reduces computation and memory cost, but limits how directly the model learns very long-range dependencies.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Gradient clipping\n",
        "\n",
        "When gradients explode, one can rescale the gradient vector $g$ before the optimizer update:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "g \\leftarrow g\\cdot \\min\\left\\{1,\\frac{c}{\\|g\\|}\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $c>0$ is a clipping threshold.  Gradient clipping does not solve all long-memory problems, but it prevents extremely large updates from destabilizing training.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.10 RNNs versus $n$-gram models\n",
        "\n",
        "An $n$-gram model approximates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(x_t\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_{t-1})\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "P(x_t\\mid x_{t-n+1},\\ldots,x_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "It stores a discrete context of length $n-1$.  An RNN approximates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(x_t\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_{t-1})\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "P(x_t\\mid h_{t-1}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $h_{t-1}$ is a learned continuous summary of the prefix.\n",
        "\n",
        "This has advantages and disadvantages.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Model | Context representation | Strength | Limitation |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| $n$-gram | last $n-1$ tokens | transparent counts | sparse for large $n$ |\n",
        "| RNN | hidden state $h_t\\in \\mathbb R^r$ | shares statistical strength through vectors | difficult long-range optimization |\n",
        "\n",
        "The RNN is more flexible because similar contexts can produce similar hidden states.  However, this flexibility comes from nonlinear optimization, so the model is harder to analyze and train.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.11 Worked example: scalar linear RNN\n",
        "\n",
        "A scalar linear RNN is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t=ah_{t-1}+bu_t,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $u_t$ is a scalar input.  Starting from $h_0=0$, we obtain\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_1=bu_1,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_2=abu_1+bu_2,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_3=a^2bu_1+abu_2+bu_3.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "By induction,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_T=\\sum_{s=1}^T a^{T-s}b u_s.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the input from time $s$ receives weight $a^{T-s}$.  If $|a|<1$, older inputs decay.  If $|a|>1$, older inputs may be amplified.  If $a\\approx 1$, the model can maintain information for a longer time.\n",
        "\n",
        "This simple formula is one of the cleanest mathematical explanations of recurrent memory.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.12 Interactive: recurrent memory and stability\n",
