        "\n",
        "## 11.19 Common pitfalls\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Confusing token embeddings with hidden states.**  The embedding $e_t$ represents the current token.  The hidden state $h_t$ represents the prefix up to time $t$.\n",
        "2. **Forgetting parameter sharing.**  The same recurrent weights are used at every time step.\n",
        "3. **Updating in the wrong order for language modeling.**  To predict $x_t$, the model should use information from $x_1,\\ldots,x_{t-1}$, not from $x_t$ itself.\n",
        "4. **Ignoring gradient products.**  Long-range learning depends on products of Jacobians.\n",
        "5. **Assuming RNNs solve all sequence problems.**  Vanilla RNNs often struggle with long dependencies.  LSTM, GRU, and attention mechanisms were developed partly to address this problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 11.20 Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "A recurrent neural network is a sequence model built from a repeated update rule\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t=\\phi(W_hh_{t-1}+W_xe_t+b_h).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The hidden state is a learned continuous summary of the prefix.  For classification, the final state can feed a softmax classifier.  For language modeling, each hidden state predicts the next token distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "The central mathematical challenge is that training requires differentiating through many repeated compositions.  Gradients contain products of Jacobian matrices, which can vanish or explode.  This explains why vanilla RNNs are historically important but limited for long-context language tasks.  The next chapter studies gated recurrent models, which modify the recurrence to control memory more explicitly.\n",
